// Ogol -- Onboard General Operations Language, pronounced OH-gol.
//
// A small interactive language in the shape of Logo, written in sysl so that it can be linked into a
// program on a microcontroller and driven over a serial line. This package is the language itself and
// nothing else: it reads text and answers with text, and knows nothing about a board, a register or a
// terminal. What drives it is a separate program.
//
// This is the block a *consumer* writes to reach it:
//
//     dependencies {
//       ogol { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/ogol", version = "0.3.0" }
//     }
//
// The coordinate names the package, `ogol`; the module a program imports is `sh.sysl.ogol`.

package {
  name    = "ogol"
  version = "0.3.0"

  // **The oldest compiler this builds with.** `sh.sysl.parsing` states 0.0.76 as its own floor, so a
  // consumer of it cannot ask for less; the reader below adds nothing newer of its own.
  sysl = "0.0.76"
}

// **The reader is `sh.sysl.parsing`'s, not this package's.** A byte cursor, a span, a line-start
// table and a binding-power loop are the same in every grammar, and Ogol had a hand-rolled copy of
// the first two. What the coordinate buys is that the copy is gone and that a diagnostic can say
// *where* -- which is the half a 257-line cursor was never going to grow on its own.
dependencies {
  parsing { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/parsing", version = "0.2.0" }
}

// **`heap = true`, and it is a decision rather than an oversight.** An interpreter grows a token
// list, a syntax tree and a dictionary while it reads, and the sizes of all three are the user's to
// choose at the keyboard. The board this is aimed at links newlib's heap whether or not a program
// touches it, so the capability costs nothing that is not already spent -- and asking for it is what
// lets a name be a `string` and a body be a `Buf`, rather than an offset into a fixed arena.
requires {
  heap = true
}
